        // What kids build
        section#builds.page-section
            .container.px-4.px-lg-5
                .row.justify-content-center
                    .col-lg-8.text-center
                        h2.mt-0 What Your Child Will Build
                        hr.divider
                        p.text-muted.mb-5 Every lesson ends with something that works, and that they want to show you. Here are some of the projects our students make.
                .row.g-4
                    +buildCard('hammer', 'Whack-a-Mole', 'Scratch', 'Moles pop up at random and you click fast to score. Their first real game, built while learning about timing and randomness.')
                    +buildCard('music-note-beamed', 'Tap Piano', 'Scratch', 'Click the keys and play a tune. Events, sounds and costumes, and plenty of proud demos at home.')
                    +buildCard('controller', 'Space Shooter', 'MakeCode Arcade', 'A proper arcade shooter with lives and a score, which they can load onto a handheld micro:bit game console and play.')
                    +buildCard('grid-3x3-gap', 'Word Guess (Wordle)', 'Python', 'Their own version of the word game everyone plays, written in real Python code.')
                    +buildCard('key', 'Secret Codes', 'Python', 'Encrypt and crack secret messages with a Caesar cipher, and find out how computers turn letters into numbers.')
                    +buildCard('trophy', 'Spark Arena', 'Python bots', 'Write a bot, then watch it battle classmates\' bots in Tron, Maze and Pond tournaments on the big screen.')

        // What a lesson looks like
        section#lessons.page-section.bg-light
            .container.px-4.px-lg-5
                .row.justify-content-center
                    .col-lg-8.text-center
                        h2.mt-0 What a Lesson Looks Like
                        hr.divider
                        p.text-muted.mb-5 A 45-minute lesson with a maximum of three students. At least half of every lesson is your child at the keyboard making something, not watching.
                .row.g-4
                    .col-md-6.col-lg-3
                        .lesson-step.h-100
                            .lesson-num 1
                            h3.h5 One new idea
                            p.text-muted.mb-0 Never two at once. The first exercise is so easy that every child gets it right. Confidence first.
                    .col-md-6.col-lg-3
                        .lesson-step.h-100
                            .lesson-num 2
                            h3.h5 Small steps up
                            p.text-muted.mb-0 We add one small thing at a time, so kids can see themselves getting better as they go.
                    .col-md-6.col-lg-3
                        .lesson-step.h-100
                            .lesson-num 3
                            h3.h5 Make it yours
                            p.text-muted.mb-0 The main event. Each child builds their own version, such as a game or a handy tool. This is where most of the lesson goes.
                    .col-md-6.col-lg-3
                        .lesson-step.h-100
                            .lesson-num 4
                            h3.h5 Stretch or practise
                            p.text-muted.mb-0 Fast finishers get a harder challenge on the same idea. Others get another easy step. No one is rushed or held back.

        // How much in a term
        section#term.page-section
            .container.px-4.px-lg-5
                .row.justify-content-center
                    .col-lg-8.text-center
                        h2.mt-0 How Much Can They Learn in a Term?
                        hr.divider
                        p.text-muted.mb-5 A term is about 10 weekly lessons. Where your child starts depends on what they already know, not just their age. Older beginners don't restart from blocks. Here's what one term looks like at each stage.
                .row.g-4
                    +termCard('Stage 1', 'First code', 'Scratch · usually Year 3', ['Make things happen in order and on cue', 'Make characters talk to each other', 'Use randomness and if/else to make games unpredictable', 'Store and use items in a list'], 'Whack-a-Mole, Tap Piano, and a finished game for their portfolio.')
                    +termCard('Stage 2', 'Blocks to real code', 'MakeCode Arcade · usually Years 3–4', ['Build arcade games with blocks', 'Flip to the Python view and edit real code', 'Use variables, true/false and functions', 'Ship a game to their portfolio'], 'Catch the Falling Items, Space Shooter, playable on a micro:bit console.')
                    +termCard('Stage 3', 'Python', 'Python · usually Years 4–6', ['Write Python from a blank file without fear', 'Use variables, input and if/elif/else', 'Read an error message calmly and fix it', 'Start using loops and lists'], 'a trading card maker, a calculator and a quiz.')
                    +termCard('Stage 4', 'Building skills', 'Python · usually Year 7+', ['Work with grids, records and files', 'Search and sort, and count the steps', 'Plan with pseudocode and test cases', 'Prepare for coding competitions (optional)'], 'Secret Codes, a leaderboard, and Spark Arena bots.')
                .row.justify-content-center.mt-5
                    .col-lg-10
                        .milestones
                            .row.g-4.text-center
                                .col-md-4
                                    .milestone-when After one term
                                    p.mb-0 They'll show you a program they built themselves. You'll hear them ask, "What if I made it do this instead?"
                                .col-md-4
                                    .milestone-when After one year
                                    p.mb-0 They'll fix their own bugs without asking, and have made something they're genuinely proud of.
                                .col-md-4
                                    .milestone-when After three years
                                    p.mb-0 They'll read and write the same kind of code professional programmers use, and have at least one competition certificate.
                        p.text-muted.text-center.small.mt-4.mb-0 We go deep, not fast: most children spend one to two years at each stage, practising each idea until it feels easy.
