        // FAQ
        section#faq.page-section
            .container.px-4.px-lg-5
                .row.justify-content-center
                    .col-lg-8
                        h2.text-center.mt-0 Questions Parents Ask
                        hr.divider
                        #faqAccordion.accordion.mt-5
                            +faq('bored', 'Will my child get bored? Is it just typing code?')
                                p.mb-0 Not likely. Every lesson is built around making something, like a game, an animation or a quiz. We learn one new idea, then your child spends most of the lesson building their own version of it. Kids who just wanted to play games usually find out they love making them.
                            +faq('term', 'How much can my child learn in one term?')
                                p.mb-0
                                    | It depends where they start. See 
                                    a(href='#term') what a term looks like at each stage
                                    | . We'd rather your child truly masters a few ideas than rushes through many, so they feel capable and keep coming back. After the trial you'll get a clear plan for their first term.
                            +faq('everything', 'My child is interested in a bit of everything. Where do we start?')
                                p.mb-0 That's completely normal at this age, and it's what the trial is for. Your child builds something quick and fun while Alvin sees what they already know and what lights them up. From there we suggest a starting point and the kind of projects they'll enjoy most.
                            +faq('robotics', 'Is it games, robotics, or "real" programming?')
                                p.mb-0 It's real programming, taught mostly through games. Kids build games and programs in Scratch, MakeCode Arcade and Python. Some students load their MakeCode games onto a handheld micro:bit game console. We don't currently run a robotics program.
                            +faq('experience', 'My child has never coded, or already did Scratch at school. Is that OK?')
                                p.mb-0 Both are fine. Complete beginners start with friendly blocks. Kids with some experience, and older beginners, start further along, often straight into Python. The trial includes a quick placement check so nobody repeats what they already know.
                            +faq('deposit', 'Why is the trial $20?')
                                p.mb-0 The $20 reserves a 45-minute session for your child, and it's credited in full to your first term when you enrol. If plans change, cancel at least 2 days ahead for a full refund, or reschedule once at no cost if something comes up last minute, like illness.
                            +faq('fees', 'How much are classes, and when are they?')
                                p.mb-0 Classes are $59 per 45-minute lesson with at most 3 students, billed per term (about 10 lessons). Active &amp; Creative Kids vouchers are accepted. Available times are shown in the booking calendar, and after the trial we'll let you know if your child can join an existing class.
                            +faq('where', 'Where are classes held? Do you teach online?')
                                p.mb-0 All classes are in person at 5 Glen Abbey St, Rouse Hill. We don't currently teach online. A laptop is optional but recommended, so your child's projects stay with them.

